{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["And Pharaoh answered him <b>לְמָחָר</b> (tomorrow), etc. And it is difficult, for even though ״the soul of the wicked desires evil״ (Proverbs 21:10) — this is specifically regarding a matter whose evil is not recognizable to a person except through reasoning, but regarding something like this, there is no person who would desire to remain in mortal danger, as indeed he himself called it: ״וְיָסֵר מֵעָלַי אֶת הַמָּוֶת״ (and remove this death from me). And it would seem possible to say that upon seeing the alacrity of Moshe, who said ״לְמַתַי אַעְתִּיר״ (when shall I plead), etc., the eye of his intellect was deceived — for Moshe said this because he recognized that the appointed time of the plague's end was approaching and from then on there would no longer be dominion to the Elohim of the Hebrews, and the plague would cease on its own — and Moshe contrived, heaven forbid, because he said in his mind that Pharaoh would certainly be eager to remove the affliction quickly; therefore he said ״הִתְפָּאֵר״ (boast), and from the simple reading it would mean at this very time and hour. Therefore he endured the distress of that interval to ascertain the matter — whether deception was involved — and he said <b>לְמָחָר</b> (tomorrow)." "<b>Rather</b>, according to our approach we have no need for this, for he wished to test the boastfulness of Moshe, who said that he would pray and attribute in his prayer to his Elohim that He would hear his prayer now and not act until tomorrow — and there is no such quality even among star- and constellation-worshippers. And he said <b>לְמָחָר</b> — meaning that he should pray now and say in his prayer that tomorrow He should remove, etc. The wicked one intended to intensify the test, that he would pray to remove and it would not be removed until tomorrow. And Moshe answered him concerning this and said: ״כִּדְבָרְךָ נַעֲשֶׂה לְמַעַן תֵּדַע כִּי אֵין כַה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ״ — that He accepts our prayer with its conditions, along the lines of what it says (Deuteronomy 4:7) ״כִּי מִי וְגוֹ׳ כַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ בְּכָל קָרְאֵנוּ אֵלָיו״, and therefore he went out immediately and prayed to HaShem. And one need not worry and say: by what would Pharaoh verify the matter, that he prayed then and not tomorrow at the time of need? Behold, since he would not pray within the city, they would know the time of his prayer, and when he went out on that same day and not tomorrow — behold, the sign is verified. And this is what it says: <b>וַיֵּצֵא מֹשֶׁה</b> etc., <b>מֵעִם פַּרְעֹה וַיִּצְעַק מֹשֶׁה</b> etc. — meaning, he cried out two things: one, to remove them, and the second, to remove them tomorrow and not earlier. And therefore he was precise in saying ״עַל דְּבַר הַצְּפַרְדְּעִים״ (concerning the matter of the frogs), and did not say ״וַיִּצְעַק וְגוֹ׳ לְהָסִיר הַצְּפַרְדְּעִים״, for then it would be understood that his prayer was about the removal alone without the time condition. ״וַיַּעַשׂ ה׳ כִּדְבַר מֹשֶׁה״ — meaning, at the time that he specified to him; ״וַיָּסַר״ etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:01.456687265Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}